        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "85d468ca-4ece-54c8-9fc5-65e1fdf33ea1",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Plants: Crocosmia, Cimicifuga\n- Plant families: Iridaceae, Ranunculaceae\n- Places: southern Africa, eastern Africa, South Africa, Sudan, Madagascar, Northern Hemisphere\n- Topics: plant taxonomy, native ranges\n\nFacts:\n- In 1851, J. E. Planchon described Crocosmia as a small genus of flowering plants in the iris family Iridaceae.\n- Crocosmia is native to grasslands in southern and eastern Africa, with a range extending from South Africa to Sudan.\n- One Crocosmia species is endemic to Madagascar.\n- Cimicifuga, also called bugbane or cohosh, was a genus of 12 to 18 flowering plant species in the family Ranunculaceae.\n- Cimicifuga was native to temperate regions of the Northern Hemisphere.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
